<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bugey</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bugey is a historical region in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ain">Ain department</a> of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, between the Jura mountains and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a>, with Belley as its chief town. Long held by the counts and dukes of Savoy, it was ceded to France with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bresse">Bresse</a> and Gex by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> in 1601 in exchange for the Marquisate of Saluzzo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bugey.jpg" alt="Bugey" width="320" height="195" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rolf Süssbrich · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grand_Colombier,_Ain,_France.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Bugey lies between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, in a loop of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> in the south-east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ain">Ain department</a>. It takes in the foothills of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jura-mountains">Jura Mountains</a>, and its highest point is the Grand Colombier. The region is divided into two parts, Haut Bugey and Bas Bugey, and its inhabitants are called Bugistes or Bugeysiens.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> bounds the Bugey to the south and east and the Ain river to the west. Its northern limit has never been settled. In 1867 Baron Achille Raverat named the Valserine as the northern border, while present-day definitions generally stretch the name over the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>. The Revermont, however, has never been counted as part of the Bugey.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Bugey was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. When Emperor Henry IV travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to submit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-vii">Pope Gregory VII</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matilda-of-tuscany">Matilda of Tuscany</a> at Canossa, he needed passage through the lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide-of-susa">Adelaide of Susa</a>, marchesa of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>. In return for her permission and support, he gave her the Bugey. From then on it belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a>, which held it until the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> of 1601 transferred it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In the countryside of the Bugey, and in the neighbouring Valromey and Chautagne, people spoke Savorêt, a dialect of the Arpitan language, in everyday life until the 1970s. The region's name is also the name of its wine, sold under the appellation Bugey AOC.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bugey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Charles Emmanuel I seized the Marquisate of Saluzzo in 1588 and kept it by the Treaty of Lyon in 1601, ceding Bresse and <b>Bugey</b> to France, but his night assault on Geneva, the Escalade of 1602, failed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Duchy of Savoy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The manuscript was flown into occupied France on 25 July 1943 by d'Astier and Jean-Pierre Lévy, in a Lockheed Hudson piloted by Hugh Verity that landed near Ambérieu-en-<b>Bugey</b>, and the words were printed in September 1943 in the first number of the clandestine review Les Cahiers de Libération.</blockquote><a href="/songs/chant-des-partisans">Chant des Partisans</a></li></ul></section>
